 into effect in Europe it's called  gdpr and that means that in Europe your  data belongs to you but in the United  States your data belongs to all these  companies that are collecting it and  they can do with it basically whatever  they want and that's a shift that we  have to make you can see that's about to  happen in California where I'm from  there is a  the statewide privacy law on that it's  moving its way to voters but what we  need is a national privacy law and that  will really not just protect the tech  industry it's gonna protect all the  consumers ultimately it's gonna protect  our kids which is really what this is  all about because look we we know that  all these companies are now looking to  bring kids into their into their social  networks as well isn't their business  model built though on selling some of  our personal data or marketing it it's  so rare to default upon or for somebody  in your position to be calling for  regulation yeah look I'm trying to  protect our industry I see the huge  advancements that are happening every  day in our industry it's amazing I mean  you could see what just happened last  week with Google duplex that was the  most amazing AI technology I've seen  it's indistinguishable from human being  when you're talking to it many people in  the computer industry feel that it  passed the Turing test that means wow is  that a human or is that a computer so if  we're at that point we need to start to  have better regulation and greater  controls the Europeans understand that  it's time for the Americans to  understand that too can I just go back  to the National privacy law what would  that do for me how would that work in  practice for me with my personal  information that would mean that  companies would have to fully disclose  how they collect your information use  your information and you'd have a right  to be forgotten so that if you want all  your information deleted you can hit  that button and be assured that your  data is gone forever you are known as an  activist CEO you know you certainly have  critics and supporters and one of the  one of the issues you've taken on is  gender equality which you are loved by  women in this country for that because  you've taken this by both hands and say  look you can't be a CEO really and not  take on gender equality well you're  absolutely right and you know that  everywhere I go I try to bring you know  25 CEOs together at a time we're at a  dinner table like I did last night with  you and say to them look we all have to  focus on critical issues that are so  important right now and one of them is  gender equality and gender equality is  number one equal advancement equal  opportunity but you know gender equality  also means equal pay which means that  men and women need to be paid the same  for the same work and see  those need to be held accountable for  making sure that they are equally paying  men and women and also it's about  preventing sexual harassment you can't  have gender equality without knowing  that you've got psychological safety in  your work in your workplace and that you  can you know work together within your  own company though you walk in you see a  meeting full of white guys and you just  said hey what's wrong with this picture  that you're even working to change that  at your own company well that's exactly  right I mean I see that every single day  we're and that's how I got involved I  said how are we going to change how do  we gonna improve our work with for  example women and that's why we've  improved the number of women that we  have in our company but also why we've  now spent more than nine million dollars  to balance out our pay scales to make  sure that we are paying men and women  equally you know that's something that  every CEO can do with one button you  know we don't we don't run our business  on three by five cards right you know  that Gail yeah we use computers that  means we can say oh we know exactly how  everyone is being paid you did that  internal audit essentially and we're  surprised by what you found even in your  own company what have other CEOs said to  you perhaps privately about why they  haven't done what you've done well the  vast majority of CEOs are afraid to push  that button because you don't really  know what's gonna come out of the system  and it's a big leap of faith but it's a  leap of faith we all have to make  because look we're all trying to improve  our workplaces you know Salesforce was  just awarded the number one best place  to work by Fortune magazine in the world  yeah very proud of that  and you know the reason why we were able  to get there is look it's gender  equality is extremely important all  aspects of a quality important diversity  inclusion is it's critical LGBTQ  equality is critical you know the last  time we were here I was talking about  you know what we were doing in Indiana  and in Georgia and you know that even  the environment is equality we talked  about that last night where we've got to  be thinking and CEOs have to take  responsibility for their products when  it comes to it I'll bet environment  Joe's it's a-okay mr. Binney I'll stop  talking we got a business to run here  but I think it's good you're speaking up  thank you very much yeah thank you for  coming always a pleasure to  thank you thank you

